      "text": "\n<think>\nHere's a thinking process:\n\n1.  **Analyze User Input:**\n   - **Context:** Tuning an Intel XPU inference server.\n   - **Key Observations (repeated many times):** \n     - Stable baseline decoding\n     - Prompt-sensitive speculative acceptance\n     - Graph capture bucket sensitivity\n     - Need to preserve exact output quality while improving single-request speed\n   - **Question:** Summarize likely bottlenecks and propose an ordered plan.\n   - **Constraints:** \n     - Concise engineering analysis\n     - Concrete next steps\n     - Technical, no marketing language\n     - At least eight dense numbered paragraphs\n     - Ordered plan\n\n2.  **Deconstruct Key Observations & Map to Bottlenecks:**\n   - *Stable baseline decoding:* Kernel execution is consistent, memory bandwidth/latency likely not the primary bottleneck for standard paths. Compute-bound or memory-bound depending on model size, but stability suggests good baseline utilization.\n   - *Prompt-sensitive speculative acceptance:* Speculative decoding performance varies significantly with prompt characteristics. This points to KV cache management, attention mechanism overhead, or speculative token validation bottlenecks that are highly dependent on input length/structure.\n   - *Graph capture bucket sensitivity:* Dynamic shapes cause frequent graph recompilation or suboptimal kernel selection. Bucketing strategy is likely too coarse/fine, causing either excessive capture overhead or poor kernel specialization.\n   - *Preserve exact output quality while improving single-request speed:* No approximation allowed (e.g., no quantization, no speculative sampling that changes distribution). Must rely on architectural/execution optimizations, memory layout, kernel fusion, or scheduling improvements.\n\n3.  **Identify Likely Bottlenecks (Intel XPU context):**\n   - Memory bandwidth/latency (KV cache, weight loading, attention computation)\n   - Graph capture/compilation overhead (dynamic shapes, bucketing)\n   - Speculative decoding validation overhead (mismatched token acceptance rates, kernel launch latency)\n   - Scheduling/queue management (single-request latency vs throughput)\n   - Kernel specialization/fusion inefficiencies on XPU architecture (Xe/ARC)\n   - Attention mechanism overhead (flash attention vs standard, memory access patterns)\n\n4.  **Develop Ordered Plan (8+ dense paragraphs):**\n   Need to structure as a numbered list of 8+ paragraphs, each dense and technical, covering bottlenecks and concrete next steps in order.\n\n",
